# Unit N1-N3 content. Quiz tuple: (q, correct, [wrong x3], why)
UNITS = []

UNITS.append(dict(id="N1", part="National", title="Property Ownership, Estates & Interests",
 objectives=["Distinguish real property, personal property and fixtures","List the bundle of rights and the estates in land","Compare forms of co-ownership and common-interest ownership","Identify easements, liens, encroachments and other encumbrances"],
 lessons=[
 dict(title="Real vs. personal property; fixtures",
  body=["**Land** is the earth's surface down to the center and up into the sky, including natural attachments (trees, minerals, water). **Real estate** is land plus man-made improvements permanently attached. **Real property** is real estate plus the *rights* of ownership (the bundle of rights). **Personal property** (chattel) is movable and not attached.",
  "A **fixture** is personal property that has been attached to land or a building so that it is legally real property. Courts apply the tests that spell **MARIA**:",
  "- **M**ethod of attachment (nailed, cemented, plumbed in = fixture)",
  "- **A**daptation to the real estate (custom-fit items)",
  "- **R**elationship of the parties (owner vs. tenant; buyer vs. seller)",
  "- **I**ntention of the person who attached it",
  "- **A**greement between the parties (the strongest evidence, if in writing)",
  "**Trade fixtures** (a restaurant's equipment, a salon's chairs) are installed by a tenant for business and remain the tenant's personal property; the tenant must remove them before the lease ends and repair damage. **Emblements** (fructus industriales) are annual crops produced by cultivation; a tenant farmer generally keeps the right to harvest them even if the lease ends. Naturally growing plants (fructus naturales) go with the land."],
  key=["Fixtures are treated as real property and pass with the sale unless excluded in writing","Agreement of the parties is the strongest test; get items listed in the contract","Trade fixtures belong to the tenant; emblements belong to the farmer-tenant"],
  example="A buyer assumes the chandelier conveys. The seller removes it before closing. Because the chandelier was hard-wired (method of attachment) and the contract did not exclude it, it is a fixture and the buyer can insist it stay. The fix: list 'chandelier in dining room EXCLUDED' in the contract."),
 dict(title="The bundle of rights and estates in land",
  body=["Owning real property gives a bundle of rights: **possession, control, enjoyment, exclusion, and disposition**. These can be separated (leased, mortgaged, given as easements).",
  "An **estate** is the degree, quantity, nature and extent of interest a person has in land. Estates are either **freehold** (of indefinite duration) or **leasehold** (a fixed or periodic term).",
  "**Freehold estates**",
  "- **Fee simple absolute** — the highest, most complete form; inheritable, no limit on time.",
  "- **Fee simple defeasible** (determinable or on condition subsequent) — ownership can end if a condition is violated; reverts automatically (determinable) or only if the grantor acts (condition subsequent).",
  "- **Life estate** — ownership measured by a person's life (their own or another's, *pur autre vie*). The **remainderman** takes at the end; if the grantor keeps the future interest it is a **reversion**.",
  "**Leasehold estates**: estate for years (fixed term), periodic tenancy (renews automatically), estate at will (no set term), estate at sufferance (holdover without consent).",
  "A leasehold is **personal property**, not real property, even though it is an interest in land. A freehold is real property."],
  key=["Fee simple absolute = most complete ownership","Life estate holder cannot will the property away; remainderman/reversioner takes at death","Leaseholds are personal property in most state classifications"],
  example="Maria deeds a lake cottage 'to my son Dan for his life, then to my daughter Eve.' Dan holds a life estate; Eve holds a remainder. Dan can use or lease the cottage but only until he dies; he cannot sell more than he owns."),
 dict(title="Forms of ownership",
  body=["**Ownership in severalty** — a single owner.",
  "**Tenancy in common** — two or more owners, undivided interests that may be unequal; each can sell, mortgage or will their share; **no right of survivorship**; unity of possession only. If a deed to several people does not say how title is held, many states presume tenancy in common.",
  "**Joint tenancy** — equal undivided interests with **right of survivorship**. Requires the four unities: **time, title, interest, possession** (T-T-I-P). Selling or transferring one's share *severs* the joint tenancy for that share, converting it to a tenancy in common for the buyer.",
  "**Tenancy by the entirety** — only for married couples in states that recognize it; survivorship; neither spouse can convey alone; creditors of just one spouse generally cannot reach it. **Community property** exists in nine states (not Mississippi).",
  "**Trusts, partnerships, corporations, LLCs** also hold title. A **land trust** keeps the owner's identity private.",
  "**Common-interest ownership**:",
  "- **Condominium** — separate fee ownership of an airspace unit plus an undivided interest in common elements; each owner gets a separate tax bill and mortgage.",
  "- **Cooperative** — a corporation owns the building; residents hold shares of stock plus a proprietary lease (generally treated as personal property).",
  "- **Planned unit development (PUD)** / **HOA** — owners hold fee title to lots with common areas run by an association; covenants and assessments run with the land.",
  "- **Timeshare** — interval ownership of use rights for periods each year."],
  key=["Joint tenancy needs T-T-I-P and survivorship","Condo = real property unit; co-op = shares + proprietary lease","Check the deed language: survivorship must be express"],
  example="Three friends buy property as joint tenants. One sells her third to a stranger. The stranger becomes a tenant in common as to that third; the other two remain joint tenants with each other (survivorship between them only)."),
 dict(title="Encumbrances: liens, easements, encroachments, restrictions",
  body=["An **encumbrance** is any claim, lien, restriction or condition that affects title or use. Some affect ownership value; some affect only use.",
  "**Liens** are money claims against property. *Voluntary* liens: mortgage/deed of trust. *Involuntary*: judgment, tax, mechanic's, IRS, special assessment. **Priority** is generally 'first in time, first in right,' but **real property tax liens and special assessments** take priority over almost everything regardless of date. A **mechanic's lien** can often relate back to when work began (state-specific).",
  "**Easements** give a non-owner the right to use land for a limited purpose. An **appurtenant** easement benefits a neighboring parcel (dominant tenement) and burdens another (servient tenement) and runs with the land. An **easement in gross** benefits a person or company (utilities). **Creation**: express grant/reservation, implication, necessity, **prescription** (open, notorious, hostile, continuous use for the statutory period). An easement ends by merger, release, abandonment, necessity ending, or (for prescriptive) non-use as provided by law.",
  "A **license** is permission to enter, revocable, personal, and not an interest in land.",
  "**Encroachment** — a structure intrudes on a neighbor's land. A survey finds it. **Deed restrictions/covenants (CC&Rs)** limit use and run with the land; violations are enforced by injunction or damages; **zoning** (public) beats looser private covenants but a stricter covenant still applies.",
  "**Water rights**: *riparian* rights belong to owners of land bordering flowing water; *littoral* rights belong to owners bordering lakes/seas/oceans. *Prior appropriation* is used in the arid West. **Accretion** (gradual addition by water), **erosion**, **avulsion** (sudden loss) and **reliction** (water recedes) change boundaries differently — accretion and reliction add to the owner; avulsion does not change the boundary."],
  key=["Taxes and assessments get priority over earlier mortgages","Easement appurtenant runs with the land; in gross does not benefit a parcel","Prescription requires open, notorious, hostile, continuous use"],
  example="A utility pipeline crosses a farm under a recorded easement in gross. The farm is sold. The new owner takes subject to the easement because it is recorded and burdens the land."),
 ],
 quiz=[
 ("A chandelier hard-wired into a dining room ceiling is most likely a:", "fixture", ["trade fixture","emblement","chattel"], "Method of attachment makes it a fixture unless the contract excludes it."),
 ("The strongest evidence of whether an item is a fixture is the:", "written agreement of the parties", ["cost of the item","age of the item","color match"], "A written agreement overrides most other fixture tests."),
 ("Which estate lasts for the lifetime of a person?", "life estate", ["estate for years","fee simple absolute","estate at will"], "A life estate is measured by a life."),
 ("Right of survivorship is characteristic of:", "joint tenancy", ["tenancy in common","severalty","a periodic tenancy"], "Joint tenants' shares pass automatically to surviving owners."),
 ("The four unities required for a joint tenancy are:", "time, title, interest, possession", ["time, tenure, intent, price","title, taxes, interest, possession","time, title, income, partition"], "T-T-I-P."),
 ("A tenant installs display cases to run a shop. At lease end they are:", "the tenant's trade fixtures and removable", ["the landlord's real property","emblements","fixtures that must stay"], "Trade fixtures remain the tenant's property if removed on time."),
 ("Which lien would generally have priority over a mortgage recorded years earlier?", "unpaid real property taxes", ["a judgment lien","a mechanic's lien filed last week","a homeowner association's voluntary pledge"], "Tax liens and special assessments have super-priority."),
 ("A neighbor uses a path openly, continuously and without permission for the full statutory period. They may gain:", "an easement by prescription", ["a license","an easement in gross by grant","a life estate"], "Prescriptive easement from adverse use."),
 ("An owner of a condominium unit holds:", "fee title to the unit and an undivided interest in common elements", ["shares of stock and a proprietary lease","a leasehold only","an easement in gross"], "That distinguishes condos from co-ops."),
 ("Riparian rights belong to owners of land bordering:", "a flowing stream or river", ["an ocean","any public road","a lake only"], "Littoral applies to still water/oceans."),
 ("A gradual build-up of soil by water deposit is:", "accretion", ["avulsion","erosion","reliction"], "Accretion adds to the owner's land."),
 ("Revocable permission to enter someone's land is a:", "license", ["easement","lien","encroachment"], "A license is personal and revocable."),
 ("Tenancy by the entirety is limited to:", "married couples where recognized", ["any two relatives","business partners","three or more owners"], "Needs marital unity."),
 ("An estate that ends if a stated condition is violated is a:", "fee simple defeasible", ["fee simple absolute","life estate pur autre vie","leasehold"], "Defeasible fee can terminate."),
 ("A leasehold estate is generally classified as:", "personal property", ["real property","a freehold","a fee"], "Leaseholds are personal property."),
 ("Annual crops produced by cultivation are:", "emblements", ["fructus naturales","trade fixtures","reliction"], "Emblements stay with the farmer-tenant."),
 ]))

UNITS.append(dict(id="N2", part="National", title="Land Use Controls, Descriptions & Environmental Issues",
 objectives=["Explain police power, zoning and nonconforming use","Read the three legal description systems","Identify key environmental hazards and disclosure duties"],
 lessons=[
 dict(title="Public controls: police power, zoning, building codes",
  body=["Government controls land use through four powers: **police power** (health, safety, morals, general welfare), **eminent domain** (taking for public use with just compensation; *condemnation* is the process), **taxation**, and **escheat** (property reverts to the state when an owner dies without heirs or will).",
  "**Zoning** divides land into districts (residential, commercial, industrial, agricultural). It is an exercise of police power and requires no compensation. Related tools:",
  "- **Nonconforming use (grandfathering)** — a pre-existing use that no longer fits new zoning may continue, but generally can't be expanded or rebuilt after major destruction.",
  "- **Variance** — permission to deviate from a zoning rule due to hardship (e.g., setback).",
  "- **Special-use / conditional-use permit** — allowed use subject to conditions (a school in a residential zone).",
  "- **Rezoning** — change of district via legislative process; **spot zoning** (singling out one parcel for benefit) is generally illegal.",
  "- **Subdivision regulations**, **building codes**, **certificates of occupancy**, and **impact fees**.",
  "A **regulatory taking** can require compensation if regulation deprives the owner of essentially all economic use."],
  key=["Zoning = police power; eminent domain requires just compensation","Nonconforming use can continue; variance is a deviation; spot zoning is illegal","Escheat applies when there are no heirs"],
  example="A shop operating legally before a residential rezoning may continue as a nonconforming use but cannot double its floor area."),
 dict(title="Legal descriptions and surveys",
  body=["A legal description must identify the parcel uniquely. Street address is not a legal description. Three methods:",
  "- **Metes and bounds** — starts at a **point of beginning (POB)**, follows distances and compass bearings, and returns to the POB. Used in the original colonies, Texas, and elsewhere; can use monuments (trees, stakes, roads).",
  "- **Rectangular (government) survey system** — grid of **principal meridians** (north–south) and **base lines** (east–west). **Ranges** are 6-mile columns east/west of the meridian; **townships** are 6-mile rows north/south of the base line; a **township** = 6×6 miles = 36 sections. A **section** = 1 mile × 1 mile = **640 acres**; numbered 1–36 in a serpentine pattern starting at the NE corner (1) and ending at the SE corner (36). Quarter section = 160 acres; a 'N½ of the SE¼' is 80 acres.",
  "- **Lot, block and tract (recorded plat)** — refers to a recorded subdivision plat map; common in urban areas.",
  "Mississippi land was surveyed under the rectangular system from several principal meridians (for example Choctaw, Chickasaw, St. Stephens and Washington), so government-survey descriptions are common for rural parcels, while subdivisions use recorded plats.",
  "**Datum / benchmark** are elevation references. A **survey** shows boundaries, easements, encroachments and improvements. **Title insurance** commonly excepts matters a survey would show unless a survey is provided.",
  "**Calculating from a legal description**: read from the end backward. 'The W½ of the NE¼ of the SW¼' of a section: start at 640 → SW¼ = 160 → NE¼ of that = 40 → W½ = 20 acres."],
  key=["A section is 640 acres; a township is 36 sections","Read government descriptions from the end backward","A metes-and-bounds description must close at the POB"],
  example="How many acres is the S½ of the NW¼ of the SE¼ of Section 12? 640 ÷ 4 = 160 (SE¼); ÷ 4 = 40 (NW¼); ÷ 2 = 20 acres."),
 dict(title="Environmental issues and hazards",
  body=["Agents are **not experts** on environmental hazards but must disclose known material facts and recommend inspection by qualified professionals.",
  "- **Lead-based paint** — homes built before **1978**. Federal law requires sellers/landlords to give the *Protect Your Family from Lead in Your Home* pamphlet, disclose known lead hazards, provide available reports, and (for sales) give buyers **10 days** (unless otherwise agreed) to inspect. The contract carries a disclosure/acknowledgment. Penalties are significant; agents share responsibility.",
  "- **Asbestos** — fibers in old insulation, tile, pipe wrap; dangerous when friable/disturbed; encapsulation or professional abatement.",
  "- **Radon** — radioactive gas from soil; tested in picocuries per liter; EPA action level is **4 pCi/L**; mitigation with sub-slab depressurization.",
  "- **Mold** — moisture control is key; remediation by professionals.",
  "- **Underground storage tanks (USTs)**, **PCBs**, **urea-formaldehyde** insulation, **methamphetamine lab residue**, **Chinese drywall**, and **wetlands** (regulated by the Army Corps/state).",
  "- **CERCLA / Superfund** — strict, joint-and-several, retroactive liability for current and past owners and operators for contaminated sites; the **innocent landowner/bona fide prospective purchaser** defense depends on due diligence — a **Phase I Environmental Site Assessment** (records, history, site visit; no sampling). Phase II samples and tests.",
  "- **Flood**: FEMA flood zones; federally regulated lenders require flood insurance in Special Flood Hazard Areas (zones beginning with A or V). Details in the Gulf Coast unit."],
  key=["Pre-1978 housing → lead disclosure and 10-day inspection window","EPA radon action level = 4 pCi/L","CERCLA liability is strict, joint and several, and retroactive"],
  example="A 1965 house is listed. The listing agent must hand the buyer the lead pamphlet and the seller's disclosure before the buyer is obligated under the contract."),
 ],
 quiz=[
 ("Zoning is an exercise of the government's:", "police power", ["eminent domain","escheat","taxation"], "Zoning regulates for public welfare."),
 ("A pre-existing use that no longer matches new zoning is a:", "nonconforming use", ["variance","spot zoning","special-use permit"], "Grandfathered use."),
 ("A deviation from zoning because of hardship is a:", "variance", ["rezoning","condemnation","setback"], "Variance."),
 ("When an owner dies with no will and no heirs, the property:", "escheats to the state", ["is condemned","reverts to the county tax collector without process","becomes a life estate"], "Escheat."),
 ("A section contains how many acres?", "640", ["160","320","36"], "1 mile × 1 mile."),
 ("A township contains how many sections?", "36", ["16","640","24"], "6×6."),
 ("How many acres in the N½ of the SW¼ of a section?", "80", ["40","160","20"], "160 ÷ 2 = 80."),
 ("A metes-and-bounds description must:", "return to the point of beginning", ["use only street names","reference a plat","end at a river"], "It closes at the POB."),
 ("Lead-based paint disclosure applies to housing built before:", "1978", ["1968","1988","1950"], "Federal rule."),
 ("The EPA radon action level is:", "4 pCi/L", ["10 pCi/L","1 pCi/L","40 pCi/L"], "Action level."),
 ("Superfund (CERCLA) liability is:", "strict, joint and several, and retroactive", ["limited to current owners","fault-based only","prospective only"], "Broad liability."),
 ("A Phase I environmental assessment involves:", "records review and site inspection without sampling", ["soil boring and lab testing","remediation","a title search only"], "Phase II samples."),
 ("A 'taking' of property for public use requires:", "just compensation", ["no compensation","only notice","a variance"], "Eminent domain."),
 ("Spot zoning is generally:", "illegal", ["required","a variance","a nonconforming use"], "Unfair singling out of one parcel."),
 ]))

UNITS.append(dict(id="N3", part="National", title="Agency, Brokerage & Ethical Duties",
 objectives=["Define agency relationships and fiduciary duties (OLD CAR)","Distinguish seller, buyer, dual, designated agent and transaction broker","Understand disclosure, creation and termination of agency","Recognize broker compensation and Realtor® Code basics"],
 lessons=[
 dict(title="Agency basics and fiduciary duties",
  body=["**Agency** is a fiduciary relationship in which a **principal** appoints an **agent** to act on their behalf in dealings with a **third party**. The broker is the agent of the principal; the broker's salespeople are the broker's sub-agents/associates. A **customer** is a third party who is not represented; a **client** is represented. A **general agent** has broad authority; a **special agent** (nearly all real estate agents) has authority for a specific transaction; a **universal agent** acts across all matters (power of attorney).",
  "**Agency creation**: express (written/oral), implied (conduct), ratification, estoppel. Written listing agreements are required to enforce a commission in most states, and Mississippi now requires a written brokerage agreement before listing or submitting an offer.",
  "**Fiduciary duties — OLD CAR**:",
  "- **O**bedience — follow lawful instructions",
  "- **L**oyalty — put the principal's interests first; no secret profit",
  "- **D**isclosure — tell the principal all material facts",
  "- **C**onfidentiality — keep the principal's confidential information (price flexibility, motives) even after the relationship ends",
  "- **A**ccounting — account for all money and documents",
  "- **R**easonable care and skill",
  "**Duties to all parties (including customers)**: honesty and fair dealing, disclosure of known material facts that affect value or desirability (**latent defects**), avoiding misrepresentation, compliance with fair housing and other laws. **Puffing** (opinion/exaggeration, e.g., 'best house on the block') is allowed; **misrepresentation** (false statement of material fact) is not. Negligent misrepresentation or fraud can create liability even if unintentional."],
  key=["OLD CAR = Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, Reasonable care","Clients get fiduciary duties; customers get honesty and fair dealing","Puffing is okay; misrepresentation is not"],
  example="A buyer's agent learns the buyer will pay $20,000 over list. Telling the listing agent would breach confidentiality and loyalty."),
 dict(title="Types of brokerage relationships",
  body=["- **Seller's (listing) agent** represents the seller; the buyer is a customer.",
  "- **Buyer's agent** represents the buyer; may be paid by the seller via cooperating-broker compensation or by the buyer, as agreed in writing.",
  "- **Dual agency** — one broker (or agent) represents both buyer and seller in the same transaction. Requires **informed written consent** from both. Fiduciary duties are limited: the dual agent cannot advocate for either on price or reveal confidential bargaining information.",
  "- **Designated agency** — the broker designates one affiliated agent for the seller and another for the buyer; each is fiduciary to their own client while the broker supervises both.",
  "- **Transaction broker / facilitator** (non-agent) — assists both parties without fiduciary duties; owes limited duties (honesty, accounting, disclosure of material facts). Recognized in many states; **Mississippi's treatment is covered in the state unit**.",
  "- **Subagency** — a broker cooperating with a listing broker, owing fiduciary duties to the listing principal; now less common because of buyer-agency. Listing agents may not offer subagency without the seller's authorization.",
  "**Disclosure**: agency disclosure must be made at first substantive contact with a prospective buyer or seller, before confidential information is shared.",
  "**Undisclosed dual agency** is a serious violation; commissions can be forfeited and the license disciplined."],
  key=["Dual agency requires informed written consent","Designated agent keeps each client's confidences within the same brokerage","Disclose agency at first substantive contact"],
  example="A salesperson at ABC Realty listed a house; a different ABC salesperson brings a buyer. ABC can use designated agency (if allowed) so each salesperson advocates for their own client."),
 dict(title="Listing agreements, compensation, termination",
  body=["**Listing types**:",
  "- **Open listing** — non-exclusive; seller can list with many brokers and only the broker who procures the buyer is paid; the seller who sells on their own owes no commission.",
  "- **Exclusive agency** — one broker, but the seller keeps the right to sell on their own without commission.",
  "- **Exclusive right to sell** — the broker is paid if *anyone* (including the owner) sells during the term; the most protective for the broker and the most common.",
  "- **Net listing** — the seller receives a net amount; the broker keeps the excess. Considered a conflict of interest and prohibited or discouraged in many states; if used, requires full disclosure.",
  "- **Multiple listing service (MLS)** — cooperative marketing network; a broker listing is entered and cooperating brokers receive an offer of compensation (terms changed under 2024 NAR settlement practices: offers of compensation are no longer displayed on the MLS, and written buyer-broker agreements are required before touring a home in many markets).",
  "**Elements of a valid listing**: competent parties, legal description or address, price and terms, commission, definite start and end date, owner signature(s), broker authority. A **definite expiration date** is required in most states; automatic renewals are disfavored.",
  "**Procuring cause**: the broker whose efforts started an unbroken chain of events leading to the sale generally earns the commission. A **protection (safety/extender) clause** pays the listing broker a commission if the seller sells within a period after expiration to a buyer the broker introduced.",
  "**Termination of agency**: expiration, completion, mutual agreement, revocation by the principal (may owe commission), renunciation by the agent, death/incapacity of the principal or agent, bankruptcy, destruction of the property, and breach. Revoking an exclusive listing can expose the seller to damages.",
  "**Commissions are negotiable** — never set by law; any agreement among brokers on rates is **price fixing** (Sherman Act). Also illegal: **group boycott** and **market allocation** (dividing territories or customers)."],
  key=["Exclusive right to sell pays the broker even if the owner sells","Commissions are negotiable; price fixing violates antitrust law","Listing needs a definite expiration date"],
  example="A seller on an exclusive agency listing finds her own buyer. She owes no commission; if she had signed an exclusive right to sell, she would."),
 ],
 quiz=[
 ("Which duty means putting the principal's interests above all others?", "loyalty", ["obedience","accounting","puffing"], "Loyalty."),
 ("Under OLD CAR, the 'C' stands for:", "confidentiality", ["competence","consent","compensation"], "Confidentiality."),
 ("A client differs from a customer in that the client:", "is represented by the agent", ["pays no commission","is always the buyer","is the broker"], "Clients get fiduciary duties."),
 ("An exaggerated opinion such as 'the best view in town' is:", "puffing", ["fraud","latent defect","misrepresentation"], "Puffing is not actionable."),
 ("Dual agency requires:", "informed written consent from both parties", ["only the seller's consent","no consent","a variance"], "Consent is mandatory."),
 ("The listing type that pays a commission even when the owner finds the buyer is:", "exclusive right to sell", ["open listing","exclusive agency","net listing"], "Broker is protected."),
 ("An open listing pays the commission to:", "the broker who procures the buyer", ["all brokers equally","the first broker named","the MLS"], "Procuring cause."),
 ("A net listing is:", "viewed as a conflict of interest and restricted in many states", ["the most common listing","required by the MLS","an exclusive agency"], "Risk of hidden profit."),
 ("Real estate commissions are set by:", "negotiation between the parties", ["state law","the MLS","the local board"], "Antitrust law forbids fixed rates."),
 ("Brokers agreeing to divide up sales territories is:", "market allocation, an antitrust violation", ["a lawful agreement","a listing agreement","subagency"], "Sherman Act."),
 ("An agent's authority limited to a particular transaction is a:", "special agency", ["general agency","universal agency","implied agency"], "Real estate agents are special agents."),
 ("A protection clause protects:", "the broker's commission after the listing expires", ["the buyer's deposit","the seller's equity","the lender"], "Extender clause."),
 ("Agency disclosure should be made:", "at first substantive contact", ["at closing","after the offer","when requested"], "Before confidential information is shared."),
 ("A transaction broker generally:", "owes limited duties and no fiduciary duties", ["is a fiduciary to both","represents only the seller","is a subagent"], "Facilitator."),
 ("The agency relationship ends automatically upon:", "death of the principal", ["a price reduction","an open house","an offer"], "Death/incapacity of principal."),
 ]))
